# worktree-sweep

The counterpart to `worktree-task`. That skill creates one worktree and tears down
the one you are standing in; this one sweeps every worktree on the machine that no
longer has a reason to exist.

```bash
python3 ~/.claude/tools/worktree-sweep/sweep.py            # audit, change nothing
python3 ~/.claude/tools/worktree-sweep/sweep.py --apply    # audit, then remove SWEEP-verdict worktrees
python3 ~/.claude/tools/worktree-sweep/sweep.py --repo trade-agents --apply
python3 ~/.claude/tools/worktree-sweep/sweep.py --json     # machine-readable
```

Removals are automatic under `--apply` — no per-worktree confirmation. That is
deliberate: every gate below must pass first, and a swept worktree is one
`git worktree add` from being recreated. Every removal is appended to
`~/.claude/worktree-sweep/sweep.log`.

The **local branch ref is kept** unless you pass `--prune-branches`. GitHub deletes
the remote branch on merge (verified: `amont` `fix/trust-per-worktree` 404s after
merge) while Forgejo keeps it (`default_delete_branch_after_merge: false`), so on
GitHub repos the local ref can be the last *named* handle on the pre-squash commits.
They stay reachable either way — in the object store and permanently as
`refs/pull/<n>/head` — but a branch ref is free and a 1 GB worktree is not.

## Gates — all must pass to earn a SWEEP verdict

1. Not the primary checkout, not the current working directory, not `locked`,
   no `.no-sweep` marker file inside it.
2. On a real branch (detached HEAD is never swept) that is not the base branch.
3. `git status --porcelain` is empty — no modified *and* no untracked files.
4. Has an upstream, and zero commits ahead of it (nothing unpushed).
5. A pull request for that branch exists and is **merged**.
6. A `v*` tag contains the merge commit — *unless the repo has no `v*` tags at
   all*, in which case merge is the release (see homelab below).

## The one thing not to break

A forge that cannot be reached yields **UNKNOWN**, and UNKNOWN never removes
anything. This is not paranoia, it is the observed failure mode of both forges:

- `gh` on an API timeout prints nothing and the naive read is "no PR exists".
  Measured: `cluster-vision` reported no PR on one run and `#14 MERGED` on the
  next; `amont#232` reported an empty merge commit while sitting on `main` as
  `1288b4d`.
- Forgejo's `/pulls` endpoint returns the **repo object** — a dict, not a list —
  when given the unsupported `state=all`. Parsed loosely, that is an empty PR
  list. `pr_forgejo` therefore requires an integer `number` *and* a `head` key
  before it believes it has a PR.

For a *keep* decision a false negative is harmless; for a *delete* decision it is
data loss. Never "optimise" an UNKNOWN into a HOLD-equivalent that proceeds.

## Forge specifics

- **GitHub** (`fredericrous/*`): `gh pr list --head <branch> --state all`.
- **Forgejo** (`{{ .forge.host }}`): needs *both* the mTLS client cert and the
  `~/.netrc` token (see `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`). Use `GET /pulls/{base}/{head}` —
  one request per branch, ~14s. Do **not** page `/pulls?state=closed`; against
  this forge that times out, which is how the three "missing" homelab PRs were
  first mis-read as absent.
- `default_delete_branch_after_merge` is **false** on the Forgejo repos, so a
  surviving remote branch says nothing about merge state.
- Both forges squash-merge, so `git merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD origin/main`
  is useless here — a merged branch's commits never appear on `main`. The PR API
  is the only reliable signal, and `git branch -d` will always refuse (which is why
  `--prune-branches` uses `-D`, gated on a confirmed merge).

## Release gate

Repos ship on `v*` tags, so a merged PR is not yet deployed and the tool holds
until a tag contains the merge commit. **homelab has zero `v*` tags** — it ships
via Flux on merge — so for any repo with no `v*` tags the gate is skipped and
merge counts as released. Without that carve-out homelab worktrees would never
be sweepable.

## Never

- Never pass `--force` to `git worktree remove`. If it refuses because of an
  untracked file, that file is the point — inspect it.
- Never sweep on the strength of a local heuristic (branch merged into main,
  remote branch deleted, commit subject matching a squash commit). Ask the forge.
